1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A combinational circuit has no memory. Its outputs are a function of the inputs present right now, and of nothing else.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Because there is no memory, the truth table is the circuit. Every output entry is fixed in advance and the table describes the behaviour completely.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
To read a network, label every wire with an expression and work from the inputs toward the output. The output label is the function.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The contrast is a sequential circuit: its output depends on the past as well, so its description carries states rather than rows.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Adding two one-bit numbers gives a sum and a carry. That is the half adder: sum is exclusive-OR, carry is AND.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Adding three bits - two operands and a carry in - is the full adder. Sum is one when an odd number of inputs is one; carry out when at least two are one.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Two half adders and one OR gate make a full adder: the first adds the operands, the second adds that sum to the carry in, and the two carries are OR-ed.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The check that matters: the assembled circuit agrees with the eight-row table on all eight inputs. That is a proof for a two-output circuit.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A four-bit adder is four full adders in a row. The carry out of one column is the carry in of the next, and that is the whole design.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Inside one column the carry passes through two gate levels: an AND, then the OR. So a carry that walks the whole chain costs two levels per column.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Five plus three: the carry is generated in column zero, propagates through columns one and two, and dies at column three. Four bits, one carry, answer eight.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
In an eight-bit adder with A equal to one, exactly one value of B makes the carry walk every stage: B equal to minus one, all ones.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Two facts decide a column. Generate: both bits are one, so this column makes a carry whatever arrives. Propagate: exactly one bit is one, so this column passes an arriving carry along.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Now substitute the recurrence into itself. The carry into column four is written directly in terms of the inputs, with no dependence on the carries below it.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
With gates wide enough, every carry is two levels deep, so the depth is a constant. That is the ideal, and it is not what a real gate library gives you.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Restrict every gate to two inputs and the deepest term needs the logarithm of n plus one levels. That logarithm is the answer to the GATE 2016 question.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Subtracting one bit from one bit gives a difference and a borrow: difference is exclusive-OR, borrow is NOT of the first bit AND the second.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
You do not need a subtractor. Complement the second operand and add one, and the adder does the work: A plus NOT B plus one.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The result is correct modulo sixteen for every pair of four-bit inputs, in both modes. The page checks all 512 combinations.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Overflow is not the carry out. It is the carry into the sign column exclusive-OR the carry out of it, and the two can disagree.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A multiplexer has two to the power n data inputs, n select lines and one output. The select lines choose which data input is delivered.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Three 2-to-1 switches build one 4-to-1 multiplexer: two switches choose within each pair, and the third chooses between the pairs.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Reading a multiplexer circuit is one substitution: the select lines are the variables, so each data input owns one minterm of the select variables.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Because the data inputs are constants or variables, a multiplexer is a lookup table: it can hold any function of its select lines.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The straightforward method: put the three variables on the select lines and the eight table entries on the eight data inputs.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The cheaper method: two variables on the selects and the third on the data lines, so each data input is zero, one, the variable, or its complement.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The smallest case: one select line, two data inputs. To realise T plus R, tie R to one data input, logic one to the other, and T to the select.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Count the switches before you draw: a 2 to the n-to-1 multiplexer needs 2 to the n minus one 2-to-1 switches, and a tree of them gives more select lines.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A decoder takes n inputs and drives exactly one of 2 to the power n outputs high. It is a minterm generator: output k is high exactly when the input spells k.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Because the outputs are the minterms, one OR gate over the chosen lines builds any sum of products. Decoder plus OR is a general function generator.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
An enable input turns a decoder into a selector: with the enable held low every output stays low, so the block is silent until it is chosen.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
So a 6-to-64 decoder needs nine 3-to-8 decoders: eight for the low bits and one to choose among them. That is GATE CSE 2007 Question 8.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A truth table describes the settled circuit. While the inputs change, a network with two paths that rejoin can show a value the table never contains.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Take F equal to A-bar B plus A C, with B and C both one. Then F is one for both values of A, so the output must not move at all.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Now let A fall. The term A C turns off before the term A-bar B turns on, and for one gate level the OR gate sees two zeros: a dip to zero.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The repair is the consensus term B C. It is redundant in the algebra, it holds one steady while A moves, and it is exactly the pair of adjacent ones the map already shows.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Four blocks, four jobs: the adder adds, the subtractor borrows, the multiplexer selects, the decoder enumerates. Each is a function of its inputs and nothing more.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Costs: two gate levels per column for the ripple, logarithmic depth for look-ahead with two-input gates, one level for a multiplexer output, and one decoder per eight outputs.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Five traps: the select order, the forgotten choosing decoder, the delay model, the missing adjacency check, and carry mistaken for overflow.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The rule to carry out: every combinational question is answered by naming the function, then the block, then the cost - in that order.

